Prove or disprove (with a counterexample) the following:

*Theorem.* Suppose a conductor carrying a net charge $Q$, when placed in an external electric field $vb(E)_e$, experiences a force $vb(F)$; if the external field is now reversed ($vb(E)_e arrow -vb(E)_e$), the force also reverses ($vb(F) arrow -vb(F)$).

What if we stipulate that the external field is _uniform_?
